{
  "id": "f7",
  "title": "Arrays",
  "lessons": [
    {
      "id": "f7-l1",
      "title": "DIMENSION",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "Scientists rarely have just one number. They have a week of temperatures, a hundred measurements, a table of results. An array keeps them all under one name.",
        "DIMENSION T(7) reserves seven numbered boxes: T(1), T(2) … T(7). The number in brackets is the index. FORTRAN counts from 1, just like mathematicians write x₁, x₂, x₃.",
        "The I–N rule still decides the type: T holds REAL numbers, K would hold integers. The DIMENSION card goes at the top of the deck, before the first statement that runs."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "      DIMENSION T(3)",
          "      T(1) = 12.5",
          "      T(2) = 14.0",
          "      T(3) = 9.5",
          "      WRITE (6,10) T(2)",
          "   10 FORMAT (1X, F5.1)",
          "      STOP",
          "      END"
        ],
        "output": [
          " 14.0"
        ]
      },
      "exercises": [
        {
          "id": "f7-l1-e1",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What is the first element of DIMENSION A(10)?",
          "options": [
            "A(1)",
            "A(0)",
            "A(10)",
            "A"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "FORTRAN counts from 1, like subscripts in math."
        },
        {
          "id": "f7-l1-e2",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What does DIMENSION K(20) create?",
          "options": [
            "20 boxes for integers, K(1) to K(20)",
            "20 boxes for real numbers",
            "One integer with the value 20",
            "An integer that can go up to 20"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "K starts with a letter from I to N, so all 20 elements are integers."
        },
        {
          "id": "f7-l1-e3",
          "type": "fill_blank",
          "prompt": "Make room for 7 measurements.",
          "code": [
            "      DIMENSION X(___)"
          ],
          "options": [
            "7",
            "6",
            "8",
            "X"
          ],
          "answer": "7",
          "explanation": "The number in brackets is how many elements there are: X(1) to X(7)."
        },
        {
          "id": "f7-l1-e4",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What does this deck print?",
          "code": [
            "      DIMENSION A(3)",
            "      A(1) = 2.0",
            "      A(2) = 3.0",
            "      A(3) = A(1) + A(2)",
            "      WRITE (6,10) A(3)",
            "   10 FORMAT (1X, F4.1)",
            "      END"
          ],
          "options": [
            "5.0",
            "2.0",
            "3.0",
            "1.0"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "A(1) is 2.0 and A(2) is 3.0, so A(3) = 2.0 + 3.0 = 5.0."
        },
        {
          "id": "f7-l1-e5",
          "type": "find_bug",
          "prompt": "This job stops with *ERROR*. Which card is wrong?",
          "code": [
            "      DIMENSION A(3)",
            "      A(1) = 1.0",
            "      A(4) = 2.0",
            "      WRITE (6,10) A(1)",
            "   10 FORMAT (1X, F4.1)",
            "      END"
          ],
          "answer": 2,
          "explanation": "A has only three elements. A(4) is outside the array. On the real 704 there was no error: it quietly wrote over whatever came next in memory."
        },
        {
          "id": "f7-l1-e6",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "Where does the DIMENSION card go?",
          "options": [
            "At the top, before the statements that run",
            "Right before END",
            "After the first WRITE",
            "Anywhere"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "The compiler needs to know about the array before it's used."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "f7-l2",
      "title": "Arrays and DO Loops",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "Arrays and DO loops belong together. The loop variable becomes the index: DO 10 I = 1, 5 with A(I) inside visits every element once.",
        "WRITE can loop by itself too: WRITE (6,20) (K(I), I = 1, 5) prints K(1) to K(5). That's called an implied DO. Even shorter: WRITE (6,20) K prints the whole array.",
        "To find the biggest value, take the first element as the best so far, then compare it with every other one."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "      DIMENSION K(5)",
          "      DO 10 I = 1, 5",
          "      K(I) = I * I",
          "   10 CONTINUE",
          "      WRITE (6,20) (K(I), I = 1, 5)",
          "   20 FORMAT (1X, 5I4)",
          "      STOP",
          "      END"
        ],
        "output": [
          "   1   4   9  16  25"
        ]
      },
      "exercises": [
        {
          "id": "f7-l2-e1",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What does WRITE (6,20) (A(I), I = 1, 3) print?",
          "options": [
            "A(1), A(2) and A(3)",
            "A(3) three times",
            "Only A(1)",
            "The numbers 1, 2 and 3"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "The implied DO runs I from 1 to 3 inside the WRITE list."
        },
        {
          "id": "f7-l2-e2",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What does this deck print?",
          "code": [
            "      DIMENSION K(3)",
            "      ISUM = 0",
            "      DO 10 I = 1, 3",
            "      K(I) = I",
            "      ISUM = ISUM + K(I)",
            "   10 CONTINUE",
            "      WRITE (6,20) ISUM",
            "   20 FORMAT (1X, I3)",
            "      END"
          ],
          "options": [
            "6",
            "3",
            "1",
            "12"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "K(1) + K(2) + K(3) = 1 + 2 + 3 = 6."
        },
        {
          "id": "f7-l2-e3",
          "type": "fill_blank",
          "prompt": "Set every element of A to zero.",
          "code": [
            "      DIMENSION A(10)",
            "      DO 10 I = 1, 10",
            "      A(___) = 0.0",
            "   10 CONTINUE"
          ],
          "options": [
            "I",
            "10",
            "1",
            "A"
          ],
          "answer": "I",
          "explanation": "The loop variable I walks through all ten elements."
        },
        {
          "id": "f7-l2-e4",
          "type": "order_lines",
          "prompt": "Stack the cards: fill K with 2, 4, 6 and print them.",
          "lines": [
            "      DIMENSION K(3)",
            "      DO 10 I = 1, 3",
            "      K(I) = 2 * I",
            "   10 CONTINUE",
            "      WRITE (6,20) K",
            "   20 FORMAT (1X, 3I3)",
            "      END"
          ],
          "answer": [
            "      DIMENSION K(3)",
            "      DO 10 I = 1, 3",
            "      K(I) = 2 * I",
            "   10 CONTINUE",
            "      WRITE (6,20) K",
            "   20 FORMAT (1X, 3I3)",
            "      END"
          ],
          "explanation": "DIMENSION first, then the loop fills the array, then one WRITE prints it all."
        },
        {
          "id": "f7-l2-e5",
          "type": "find_bug",
          "prompt": "This job stops with *ERROR*. Which card is wrong?",
          "code": [
            "      DIMENSION A(5)",
            "      DO 10 I = 1, 6",
            "      A(I) = 1.0",
            "   10 CONTINUE",
            "      END"
          ],
          "answer": 1,
          "explanation": "The loop goes up to 6, but A only has 5 elements. DO 10 I = 1, 5 fits the array."
        },
        {
          "id": "f7-l2-e6",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "To find the largest value in A(1) … A(N), what do you start with?",
          "options": [
            "BIG = A(1), then compare with A(2) to A(N)",
            "BIG = 0.0, always",
            "BIG = A(N), then go backwards",
            "BIG = N"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "Starting with 0.0 fails if all values are negative. The first element is always a safe start."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "f7-l3",
      "title": "Tables",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "An array can have two indexes: DIMENSION M(3,4) is a table with 3 rows and 4 columns, 12 numbers in all. M(2,3) is row 2, column 3.",
        "Two nested DO loops walk through a table: the outer one over the rows, the inner one over the columns.",
        "In memory, FORTRAN stores a table column by column: M(1,1), M(2,1), M(3,1), then M(1,2) … That's why WRITE (6,20) M prints the columns one after the other. Most later languages chose rows instead."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "      DIMENSION M(2,3)",
          "      DO 20 I = 1, 2",
          "      DO 10 J = 1, 3",
          "      M(I,J) = 10 * I + J",
          "   10 CONTINUE",
          "   20 CONTINUE",
          "      DO 30 I = 1, 2",
          "      WRITE (6,40) (M(I,J), J = 1, 3)",
          "   30 CONTINUE",
          "   40 FORMAT (1X, 3I4)",
          "      STOP",
          "      END"
        ],
        "output": [
          "  11  12  13",
          "  21  22  23"
        ]
      },
      "exercises": [
        {
          "id": "f7-l3-e1",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "How many numbers does DIMENSION M(3,4) hold?",
          "options": [
            "12",
            "7",
            "3",
            "4"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "3 rows × 4 columns = 12."
        },
        {
          "id": "f7-l3-e2",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "In the example, what is M(2,3)?",
          "options": [
            "23",
            "32",
            "13",
            "6"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "10 × 2 + 3 = 23: row 2, column 3."
        },
        {
          "id": "f7-l3-e3",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "In which order does FORTRAN keep M(2,2) in memory?",
          "options": [
            "M(1,1), M(2,1), M(1,2), M(2,2)",
            "M(1,1), M(1,2), M(2,1), M(2,2)",
            "In random order",
            "Biggest value first"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "Column by column. The first index changes fastest."
        },
        {
          "id": "f7-l3-e4",
          "type": "fill_blank",
          "prompt": "Print row I of the table, all 4 columns.",
          "code": [
            "      WRITE (6,20) (T(I,J), J = 1, ___)"
          ],
          "options": [
            "4",
            "3",
            "I",
            "12"
          ],
          "answer": "4",
          "explanation": "J walks through the columns of row I."
        },
        {
          "id": "f7-l3-e5",
          "type": "find_bug",
          "prompt": "This job stops with *ERROR*. Which card is wrong?",
          "code": [
            "      DIMENSION G(3,3)",
            "      G(1,1) = 5.0",
            "      G(2) = 7.0",
            "      WRITE (6,10) G(1,1)",
            "   10 FORMAT (1X, F4.1)",
            "      END"
          ],
          "answer": 2,
          "explanation": "G is a table, so it needs two indexes, like G(2,1)."
        },
        {
          "id": "f7-l3-e6",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What does this deck print?",
          "code": [
            "      DIMENSION G(3,3)",
            "      DO 10 I = 1, 3",
            "      G(I,I) = 2",
            "   10 CONTINUE",
            "      K = G(1,1) + G(2,2) + G(3,3)",
            "      WRITE (6,20) K",
            "   20 FORMAT (1X, I3)",
            "      END"
          ],
          "options": [
            "6",
            "9",
            "3",
            "0"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "The diagonal is G(1,1), G(2,2), G(3,3), each set to 2: 2 + 2 + 2 = 6."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
